+## Floating window
+
+While you're in a voice room (a direct message call included), if the app window is minimized or hidden to the tray, the cameras in the room, or profile photos and the speaking ring when there are no cameras, appear in a small, frameless, always-on-top window (`public/js/39-yuzen.js`, `src/lib/float-window.js`). When the window comes back, a floating window that opened by itself closes. The floating window has Microphone, Back to App and Close buttons. Opening by itself can be turned off for this frequency in the web app under Settings > Voice and Video, and the Floating Window button on the radio card always opens the window by hand.
+
+- In Electron 44 document picture-in-picture (`documentPictureInPicture`) seems to exist, but the window it opens is not visible (tried with Electron 44.5.1). So on the desktop the web app uses its own path: it gets a single-use name from the main process with `telsizDesktop.float.arm()` and calls `window.open('about:blank', name)`. The script of the app window draws the content of the window, and the camera streams stay in the same process.
+- In `setWindowOpenHandler` the main process allows only this request: it comes from the app window, the address is `about:blank` and the name is the single-use name given by `telsiz:float-arm`, valid for about 3 seconds (`telsiz-yuzen-` and 32 hex characters). At most one floating window is open at a time. The frame of the YouTube player can call `window.open` without a user gesture, but it cannot know the name. After the window opens, `did-create-window` checks again that the opener is the main frame of the app window (`webContents.opener`), and otherwise the window is destroyed.
+- The window has no preload script (no `telsizDesktop`) and its context is registered as `float`: all permissions, navigations and new windows are denied. The CSP and the sandbox are inherited from the app window.
+- The window is always on top (`setAlwaysOnTop(true, 'floating')`) and visible on all workspaces, and opens without taking focus in the bottom right corner of the app window's screen. On Windows and macOS `setContentProtection(true)` excludes the window from screen capture. Linux has no such protection, so the window shows up in a full-screen share. In a Wayland session the window cannot stay on top.
+- Its position and size are kept as `floatBounds` in `ayarlar.json`. If the saved position does not fit in the work area of the app window's screen, the window goes back to the bottom right corner.
+- The floating window closes when the app window closes, reloads or switches frequency. Back to App (`telsizDesktop.float.showMain()`) brings the app window to the front.
+
